[resource]
script = ExtResource("1")
id = "fi22n_seahawk"
display_name = "F/I-22N Seahawk"
role = "Naval Stealth Strike"
nation = "Federal Republic of Valorterra"
description = "Valorterran naval twin-seat stealth — F/I-22N Seahawk. Carrier-capable low-observable strike fighter. Fitted with MR/TRS-27A Xanadu AESA, MR/ARS-55AX RWR, and CITDS (Compatible-Integrated Tactical Defense Suite) EW/ESM.\n\nThe Seahawk's bloodline begins years before its carrier decks. In 1993, Zenith & Roscoe Group (ZRG) won Valorterra's contract to produce the land-based F/I-22 Taima, locking ZRG in as the Republic's stealth airframe house. That win pulled New Akron Federation into a shared next-step effort under the F-XX Project, with Defense Tinker Company (DTC) and Lawrence supplying propulsion, shipboard integration studies, and industrial capacity on the Akron side. By the mid-1990s the program had split into competing naval prototypes: ZRG's YF/I-22N Shark against NDOTunited's NSF. On paper, the NSF looked stronger — broader weapon compatibility, sharper speed and agility numbers — and procurement boards argued it hard. In the water and in the air, the Shark answered differently. It flew live carrier cycles, arrested recoveries, and deck handling trials; it fought through extended mock-combat packages against manned and unmanned aggressors; and it kept clearing the bars the Department of Wartime Center had set for a true fleet fighter, not just a fast demonstrator.\n\nOn 27 September 1997, the Department of Wartime Center of Valorterra, together with the Office of Military Procurement, formally selected ZRG's YF/I-22N Shark. The decision was sold publicly as more than a paper fly-off win. The same campaign that sealed the contract also staged the first open live debut of CITDS, presented as the next generation of onboard self-defense for contested maritime airspace, and a hard A2A demonstration in which the Shark killed an unmanned F-16SM test article under controlled range conditions. From that down-select the production F/I-22N Seahawk followed into fleet service.\n\nIn service the Seahawk has primarily flown for Valorterra, New Akron, Baltimore, and a wider ring of export customers who wanted a twin-seat naval stealth striker rather than a land-only stealth jet. Its lethal A2A starter kit is built around the RP-10X Angeleye — a multi-modal missile designed to deceive and defeat aircraft self-defense suites, and fully HOBS-compatible for high-off-boresight snaps in the merge. Alongside it, RP-4C ARMS covers the BVR-to-medium-range dogfight band with improved fuel burn, longer motor life, cleaner ergonomics for the crew, and updated motor servos that keep the round mobile when the fight collapses inside the horizon."
